><!----><!----><div class="bullet"></div><span class="option-text text-ds-black-500">Recall</span></div><div class="option-container full-width"><!----><!----><div class="bullet"></div><span class="option-text text-ds-black-500">Accuracy</span></div><div class="option-container full-width"><!----><!----><div class="bullet"></div><span class="option-text text-ds-black-500">F1-Score</span></div></div></button><button type="button" class="question-container btn-focus rounded-xl bg-white" data-cy="question-review-no-26" style="border-left-color: rgb(0, 201, 133);"><!----><div class="question-info-container"><!----><div class="question-text-wrapper .without-image"><div class="question-text text-ds-black-500">26. &nbsp; Which approach is commonly used to simplify rule-based induction models?</div></div></div><div class="options-container"><div class="option-container full-width"><!----><!----><div class="bullet"></div><span class="option-text text-ds-black-500">Pruning redundant or less significant rules</span></div><div class="option-container full-width"><!----><!----><div class="bullet"></div><span class="option-text text-ds-black-500">Adding more features to the dataset</span></div><div class="option-container full-width"><!----><!----><div class="bullet"></div><span class="option-text text-ds-black-500">Increasing the depth of the decision tree</span></div><div class="option-container full-width"><!----><!----><div class="bullet"></div><span class="option-text text-ds-black-500">Using more training samples</span></div></div></button><button type="button" class="question-container btn-focus rounded-xl bg-white" data-cy="question-review-no-27" style="border-left-color: rgb(0, 201, 133);"><!----><div class="question-info-container"><!----><div class="question-text-wrapper .without-image"><div class="question-text text-ds-black-500">27. &nbsp; A factory has two machines, A and B. Machine A produces 60% of the products, and machine B produces 40%. If 2% of A's products and 3% of B's products are defective, what is the probability that a randomly selected defective product came from Machine B?</div></div></div><div class="options-container"><div class="option-container full-width"><!----><!----><div class="bullet"></div><span class="option-text text-ds-black-500">0.40</span></div><div class="option-container full-width"><!----><!----><div class="bullet"></div><span class="option-text text-ds-black-500">0.50</span></div><div class="option-container full-width"><!----><!----><div class="bullet"></div><span class="option-text text-ds-black-500">0.60</span></div><div class="option-container full-width"><!----><!----><div class="bullet"></div><span class="option-text text-ds-black-500">0.75</span></div></div></button><button type="button" class="question-container btn-focus rounded-xl bg-white" data-cy="question-review-no-28" style="border-left-color: rgb(0, 201, 133);"><!----><div class="question-info-container"><!----><div class="question-text-wrapper .without-image"><div class="question-text text-ds-black-500">28. &nbsp; What is the main advantage of rule-based induction models?</div></div></div><div class="options-container"><div class="option-container full-width"><!----><!----><div class="bullet"></div><span class="option-text text-ds-black-500">They provide an interpretable set of rules for decision-making.</span></div><div class="option-container full-width"><!----><!----><div class="bullet"></div><span class="option-text text-ds-black-500">They work well with both numerical and categorical data.</span></div><div class="option-container full-width"><!----><!----><div class="bullet"></div><span class="option-text text-ds-black-500">They can generalize well without requiring large datasets.</span></div><div class="option-container full-width"><!----><!----><div class="bullet"></div><span class="option-text text-ds-black-500">Both (a) and (b)</span></div></div></button><button type="button" class="question-container btn-focus rounded-xl bg-white" data-cy="question-review-no-29" style="border-left-color: rgb(0, 201, 133);"><!----><div class="question-info-container"><!----><div class="question-text-wrapper .without-image"><div class="question-text text-ds-black-500">29. &nbsp; What does Bayes' Theorem primarily help with in probability and statistics?</div></div></div><div class="options-container"><div class="option-container full-width"><!----><!----><div class="bullet"></div><span class="option-text text-ds-black-500">Determining prior probabilities only</span></div><div class="option-container full-width"><!----><!----><div class="bullet"></div><span class="option-text text-ds-black-500">Updating probabilities based on new evidence</span></div><div class="option-container full-width"><!----><!----><div class="bullet"></div><span class="option-text text-ds-black-500">Computing standard deviation of a dataset</span></div><div class="option-container full-width"><!----><!----><div class="bullet"></div><span class="option-text text-ds-black-500">Finding mean and median in distributions</span></div></div></button><button type="button" class="question-container btn-focus rounded-xl bg-white" data-cy="question-review-no-30" style="border-left-color: rgb(0, 201, 133);"><!----><div class="question-info-container"><!----><div class="question-text-wrapper .without-image"><div class="question-text text-ds-black-500">30. &nbsp; What is the primary purpose of a confusion matrix in evaluating a classifier?</div></div></div><div class="options-container"><div class="option-container full-width"><!----><!----><div class="bullet"></div><span class="option-text text-ds-black-500">To visualize the performance of a classifier by showing correct and incorrect predictions</span></div><div class="option-container full-width"><!----><!----><div class="bullet"></div><span class="option-text text-ds-black-500">To calculate the computational complexity of the classifier</span></div><div class="option-container full-wid